Part 1 — The Linux Boot Sequence (Theory)
Understanding the full chain is critical. Here's what happens when you power on a Rocky Linux 9 machine:
scribd
+1

BIOS/UEFI — firmware initializes hardware, runs POST, locates the boot device.

GRUB2 (Grand Unified Bootloader) — reads /boot/grub2/grub.cfg, displays the kernel menu, lets you edit boot parameters.
rockylinux

Kernel loading — GRUB loads the compressed kernel (vmlinuz) and the initial RAM filesystem (initramfs) into memory.

initramfs — a temporary root filesystem that contains essential drivers (storage, filesystem, LVM) needed to find and mount the real root / partition.
danielvelasco
+1

systemd (PID 1) — the kernel hands off to systemd, which reads the default target and starts all required services in parallel.

Default target reached — system is ready (e.g., multi-user.target or graphical.target).

